-- title:
--   Extending an anemic weight-two Hecke point with U_q normalised
-- statement:
--   Let $N\ge 1$ be a natural number (nonzero) and let $S_0\subseteq S$ be finite sets of natural numbers such that every prime divisor $q$ of $N$ lies in $S$. For a set $\Sigma$ write $\mathbb{T}^{\Sigma}$ for [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 Σ`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C}\bigl(S_2(\Gamma_0(N))\bigr)$ generated by the operators `heckeTLin` attached to primes $\ell\nmid N$ with $\ell\notin\Sigma$ together with the operators `heckeULin` attached to primes $q\mid N$ with $q\notin\Sigma$; since $S_0\subseteq S$ one has $\mathbb{T}^{S}\le\mathbb{T}^{S_0}$. Let $\mathcal O'$ be a domain which is a discrete valuation ring, adically complete for its maximal ideal, with finite residue field and of characteristic zero, and let $\chi:\mathbb{T}^{S}\to\mathcal O'$ be a ring homomorphism. Then there is a type $\mathcal O''$ carrying the same structures (a characteristic-zero, maximal-ideal-adically complete discrete valuation domain with finite residue field) together with an $\mathcal O'$-algebra structure making $\mathcal O''$ a finite $\mathcal O'$-module, with $\mathcal O'\to\mathcal O''$ a local homomorphism and injective, and a ring homomorphism $\chi':\mathbb{T}^{S_0}\to\mathcal O''$ such that: $\chi'$ restricted along the inclusion $\mathbb{T}^{S}\hookrightarrow\mathbb{T}^{S_0}$ equals $\chi$ followed by $\mathcal O'\to\mathcal O''$; $\chi'(U_q)=0$ for every prime $q\notin S_0$ with $q\mid N$ and $q^2\mid N$; and for every prime $q\notin S_0$ with $q\mid N$ and $q^2\nmid N$ there exists $\beta\in\mathcal O''$ with $q=\chi'(U_q)\,\beta$ and either $\chi'(U_q)$ a unit or $\beta$ not a unit.
--
--   This is the passage from an eigenvalue system away from $S$ to one away from the smaller set $S_0$, i.e. the realisation of an anemic Hecke point as a point of the fuller Hecke algebra with the Atkin–Lehner normalisation at the primes dividing the level: $U_q$ kills the eigenvector when $q^2\mid N$, and at $q\parallel N$ the eigenvalue divides $q$ and is chosen to be the unit root whenever a unit root exists. It supplies the automorphic input for the level-raising and Frobenius-characteristic-polynomial statements that cite it, in the shape of Darmon–Diamond–Taylor, §4.2.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CuspForm_heckeAlgebra_exists_ringHom_extension_apply_U_eq_zero_and_isUnit_apply_U_or.lean

